     "<p style=\"font-size:16px;margin:0in;font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;color:rgb(33,33,33)\">Dear all,</p><p style=\"font-size:16px;margin:0in;font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;color:rgb(33,33,33)\"><br></p><p style=\"font-size:16px;margin:0in;font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;color:rgb(33,33,33)\">I am trying to better understand how the precision matrix Q is calculated in the barrier model. The eq. 8 in the barrier paper (Bakka et al. 2019) is:</p><p style=\"margin:0in;font-size:medium;font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;color:rgb(0,0,0)\">A = J - 1/8*sum(ranges[k]^2 * D[k])</p><p style=\"font-size:16px;margin:0in;font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;color:rgb(33,33,33)\"> </p><p style=\"font-size:16px;margin:0in;font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;color:rgb(33,33,33)\">In the Barrier.R, it is:</p><p style=\"font-size:16px;margin:0in;font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;color:rgb(33,33,33)\">“A &lt;- fem$I</p><p style=\"font-size:16px;margin:0in;font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;color:rgb(33,33,33)\">  for (k in 1:xi) {</p><p style=\"font-size:16px;margin:0in;font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;color:rgb(33,33,33)\">     A &lt;- A + (ranges[k]^2 / 8.0) * fem$D[[k]]</p><p style=\"font-size:16px;margin:0in;font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;color:rgb(33,33,33)\">  }”</p><p style=\"font-size:16px;margin:0in;font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;color:rgb(33,33,33)\"> </p><p style=\"font-size:16px;margin:0in;font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;color:rgb(33,33,33)\"><span style=\"color:black\">I think that matrices I and J are the same thing. Why does equation 8 use a minus, while the code uses a plus? I am not a mathematician, so please forgive me if this is too basic.</span></p><p style=\"font-size:16px;margin:0in;font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;color:rgb(33,33,33)\"><span style=\"color:black\"><br></span></p><p style=\"font-size:16px;margin:0in;font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;color:rgb(33,33,33)\"><span style=\"color:black\">Thanks,</span></p><p style=\"font-size:16px;margin:0in;font-family:Aptos,sans-serif;color:rgb(33,33,33)\"><span style=\"color:black\">Rujia</span></p>"
